{"name":"Indian Summer","hex":"#a85143","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"indian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Indian (“indigene”)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Indian (“indigenous”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/indian"},{"term":"summer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of four seasons, traditionally the second, marked by the longest and typically hottest days of the year due to the inclination of the Earth and thermal lag. Typically regarded as spanning  either the period between the summer solstice to the autumnal equinox, or the months of June, July, and August in the Northern Hemisphere and the months of December, January and February in the Southern Hemisphere.","year; used to give the age of a person, usually a young one."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spend the summer, as in a particular place on holiday."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/summer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Indian Summer (1970 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Indian Summer is a 1970 Croatian film directed by Nikola Tanhofer."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Summer_(1970_film)","wikidata":"Q3515759"},{"title":"Indian summer","blocks":[{"text":"An Indian summer is a period of unseasonably warm, dry weather that sometimes occurs in autumn in temperate regions of the Northern Hemisphere. Several sources describe a true Indian summer as not occurring until after the first frost, or more specifically the first \"killing frost.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/La_Fageda_d%27en_Jord%C3%A0_un_dia_d%27estiuet_de_Sant_Mart%C3%AD.JPG/330px-La_Fageda_d%27en_Jord%C3%A0_un_dia_d%27estiuet_de_Sant_Mart%C3%AD.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_summer","wikidata":"Q280004"},{"title":"Summer","blocks":[{"text":"Summer or summertime is the hottest and brightest of the four temperate seasons, occurring after spring and before autumn. Starting on the summer solstice, daylight hours are the longest and darkness hours are the shortest, with day length decreasing as the season progresses after the solstice. In the Northern Hemisphere, summer is usually defined as starting on June 21 or 22, and ending on September 22 or 23; and in the Southern Hemisphere, as the period between December 22 or 23 and March 20 or 21."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Field_Hamois_Belgium_Luc_Viatour.jpg/330px-Field_Hamois_Belgium_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summer","wikidata":"Q1313"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/La_Fageda_d%27en_Jord%C3%A0_un_dia_d%27estiuet_de_Sant_Mart%C3%AD.JPG/500px-La_Fageda_d%27en_Jord%C3%A0_un_dia_d%27estiuet_de_Sant_Mart%C3%AD.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/La_Fageda_d%27en_Jord%C3%A0_un_dia_d%27estiuet_de_Sant_Mart%C3%AD.JPG/1280px-La_Fageda_d%27en_Jord%C3%A0_un_dia_d%27estiuet_de_Sant_Mart%C3%AD.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/La_Fageda_d%27en_Jord%C3%A0_un_dia_d%27estiuet_de_Sant_Mart%C3%AD.JPG/500px-La_Fageda_d%27en_Jord%C3%A0_un_dia_d%27estiuet_de_Sant_Mart%C3%AD.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Indian summer day in Fageda d'en Jordà, a beech forest located in Garrotxa county, Catalonia (Spain).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALa_Fageda_d'en_Jord%C3%A0_un_dia_d'estiuet_de_Sant_Mart%C3%AD.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Mount_Norwottuck_in_Autumn.jpg/500px-Mount_Norwottuck_in_Autumn.